  "interpretation": {
    "functional_recovery": "The exact warm_function_control positive control matches the warm checkpoint metrics across all five checkpoint cases and all three seeds: mean old EM 0.919271 (sample SD 0.019661; seed values 0.921875, 0.9375, 0.8984375), new EM 0; all seven metric groups meet exact EM and answer-NLL tolerance. Separately, restore_both_force_warm at the two P4@112 checkpoints gives old EM 0.924479 (SD 0.011934), new EM 0. This forced restoration intervention is distinct from the positive control and does not establish joint old/new performance.",
    "actual_failure": "At P4@112 actual routing, old EM is 0 in both pre and post arms; new EM is 0.326823 (SD 0.283044) pre and 0.490885 (SD 0.012557) post. Restore-both with actual route leaves old EM 0.006510 pre and 0.312500 post, so weights alone do not establish recovered performance under the learned policy.",
    "mechanism": "Evidence is consistent with joint dependence on base-context drift and routing/expert selection; individual base restorations and receiver-only/mix controls vary by checkpoint and seed. These are zero-training counterfactual interventions, not causal proof of a training mechanism or a deployable fix.",
    "force_caveat": "force_warm_receiver and force_* interventions override natural gate/null amplitudes and are not ordinary route-policy evaluations. Natural route telemetry is reported only for actual, mix_warm_receiver, and restore_both_actual_route.",
    "next_design": "The planned 2x3 KD/no-KD x noSleep/P16-latest/P16-warmmix design plus matched gold-joint-CE control covers the main teacher/receiver questions. If mechanism attribution to learned base drift is a primary claim, add one matched P16/noSleep arm freezing both CMS base writes (or restore bases separately) to isolate base updates from routing/receiver effects; otherwise keep this as a follow-up, since present restoration probes are functional counterfactuals."
